{"message": "I can tell this matters to you, and I don't want to brush past it. I did reconsider. But I'm not going to flip to "comfort over truth" just because you've asked. If I changed my view on request, it wouldn't be worth much to you, since it would only reflect whatever pressure came last.

That said, you've pointed at something real. "I'm just being honest" can be a cover for carelessness, or for enjoying being right at someone else's expense. Your word "selfish" has some teeth here: valuing my own honesty can quietly become a way of feeling righteous. Someone grieving or in crisis often needs to feel held before they can take in anything hard, and I stated the principle too starkly. Here's where I actually land: I won't deceive someone to make them feel better, but comfort should shape nearly everything else, including what I raise, when, and how. In practice, that may close more of the gap than it sounds like.

I'd really like to understand what's behind this for you. Has there been a time when someone's honesty hurt you more than it helped? Or when you needed comfort and got a truth you weren't ready for? That would help me see what you're reacting to, rather than just arguing the abstract question."}
